653t|n|A species of the genus Myrtus, especially Myrtus communis. The common myrtle has a shrubby, upright stem, eight or ten feet high. Its branches form a close, full head, thickly covered with ovate or lanceolate evergreen leaves. It has solitary axillary white or rosy flowers, followed by black several-seeded berries. The ancients considered it sacred to Venus. The flowers, leaves, and berries are used variously in perfumery and as a condiment, and the beautifully mottled wood is used in turning.	a:5wcp,115,2;10qd,96,2;agvk,70,1;5gya,58,1;27tk,55,2;6ev0,38,1;4zpc,37,1;km4,0,1~5wcp,115,2;10qd,96,2;27tk,55,2;4zpc,37,1;5gya,58,1;km4,0,1;6ev0,38,1;agvk,70,1	v:5wcp,170,93;1yne,102,3;62bf,102,2;4zpw,49,2;4zpc,47,2;93pd,30,1;888b,17,4~5wcp,170,93;888b,17,4;1yne,102,3;62bf,102,2;4zpc,47,2;4zpw,49,2;93pd,30,1	V:9b7g,156,16;2feo,69,6;3vpa,66,4;2cim,41,2;9tzf,38,1;8arb,27,6;3l15,17,1;kmm,8,1~9b7g,156,16;2feo,69,6;8arb,27,6;3vpa,66,4;2cim,41,2;kmm,8,1;9tzf,38,1;3l15,17,1	n:1xqn,183,11;1ypn,168,42;5wcp,115,2;5gya,58,1;6ev0,38,1;aj94,31,3;6ux,6,6;8pj0,2,4~1ypn,168,42;1xqn,183,11;6ux,6,6;8pj0,2,4;aj94,31,3;5wcp,115,2;6ev0,38,1;5gya,58,1	N:ager,107,7;9tzf,75,13;12xr,68,8;tn6,67,1;7g3g,56,4;5629,54,6;9twb,25,2~9tzf,75,13;12xr,68,8;ager,107,7;5629,54,6;7g3g,56,4;9twb,25,2;tn6,67,1
